    {
      "id": "vav-connector",
      "order": 3,
      "title": "What vav is really doing between clauses",
      "kind": "single",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Group 1's very first lesson covered vav as “and”. That's always grammatically true, but between whole clauses vav's actual function stretches further: depending on context it can read as “but” (contrast), “so/then” (result or sequence), or simply move the narrative forward -- English usually needs a different word for each of these, but Hebrew reuses the same one letter throughout."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "ref": "Jonah.1.5",
          "surface_form": "וְיוֹנָה",
          "transliteration": "weyonah",
          "prefix_form": "וְ",
          "prefix_morphemes": [
            {
              "citation_form": "וְ",
              "transliteration": "we",
              "gloss": "and"
            }
          ],
          "base_form": "יוֹנָה",
          "suffix_form": "",
          "suffix_kind": null,
          "suffix_pgn": null,
          "lemma_citation_form": "יוֹנָה",
          "lemma_transliteration": "yonah",
          "gloss": "Jonah (proper name)",
          "highlight": "base",
          "note": "Grammatically this is still just “and” -- the same prefix group 1's very first lesson covered. But in context (everyone else is panicking on deck while this happens) it reads far more naturally as a contrastive “BUT Jonah had gone down [and lain down and was fast asleep]” -- Hebrew doesn't have a separate word for that shift, the vav just carries it."
        },
        {
          "ref": "Jonah.1.6",
          "surface_form": "וְלֹא",
          "transliteration": "welo'",
          "prefix_form": "וְ",
          "prefix_morphemes": [
